[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Network Relevancy, Priority, and Spatialization in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In large-scale multiplayer games using Unreal Engine, network bandwidth is a finite resource. Optimizing how data flows between the server and clients is crucial for performance. This tutorial covers Network Relevancy, Priority, and Spatialization to help you manage network traffic efficiently."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Network Relevancy"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Network relevancy determines whether a specific Actor is replicated to a given client connection. By default, Unreal Engine uses distance-based relevancy, but you can override this behavior."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"bAlwaysRelevant: When set to true, the Actor is always replicated to all clients, regardless of distance."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NetCullDistanceSquared: Defines the maximum distance squared at which the Actor stops being relevant to a client."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// Example in C++ Constructor to configure relevancy properties
PrimaryActorTick.bCanEverTick = true;
bReplicates = true;
bAlwaysRelevant = false;
NetCullDistanceSquared = 9000000.0f; // 3000 units"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Customizing Network Priority"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once an Actor is deemed relevant, the NetPriority variable dictates how often its properties are updated relative to other actors. Higher priority actors get bandwidth assigned first during each network tick."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default NetPriority for Pawns is usually set to 2.0 or 3.0."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Background scenery or distant interactive props should have lower priorities (e.g., 0.5 or 1.0)."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Adjusting priority dynamically
NetPriority = 4.0f;"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Spatialization and Custom Relevancy Logic"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"For advanced spatialization, you can override the IsNetRelevantFor function in your C++ class. This allows you to write custom line-of-sight or zone-based relevancy rules."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"bool AMyCustomActor::IsNetRelevantFor(const AActor* RealViewer, const AActor* ViewTarget, const FVector& SrcLocation) const
{
    // Implement custom spatial checks here
    return Super::IsNetRelevantFor(RealViewer, ViewTarget, SrcLocation);
}"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By correctly configuring bAlwaysRelevant, tweaking NetCullDistanceSquared, setting appropriate NetPriority values, and implementing custom IsNetRelevantFor logic, you can drastically reduce network congestion and improve your game's multiplayer performance."}]
